`skoro-normal` provides `Normal<B, M>`: a Gaussian on any manifold `M`, parameterized by a mean
`Q<B, M>` and a lower-triangular factor `Q<B, Tril>`. This page defines it precisely, then derives
the two things its factor offers the estimators: a whitened residual and a root of its Fisher
information.

## The law

Let $d$ be the dimension of `M`, $\Sigma = \whiten\whiten\transpose$, and $\W = \whiten^{-1}$ (the
**whitening**). $x \sim \mathcal N(\mean, \Sigma)$ on `M` means that the tangent coordinates of $x$
in the chart at the mean ([Manifolds and retractions](../manifolds/)) are Gaussian:
$$
v = x \ominus \mean \sim \mathcal N(0, \Sigma) \quad\text{in } \R^d .
$$
The term it adds to the model is the log-density of $v$:
$$
\log p(x \mid \mean, \whiten)
= -\tfrac{d}{2}\ln 2\pi \;-\; \tfrac12 \big\lVert \W (x \ominus \mean) \big\rVert^2 \;+\; \sum_{i=1}^{d} \ln\lvert \W_{ii} \rvert .
$$
The last sum is $\ln\lvert\det \W\rvert = -\tfrac12 \ln\det\Sigma$, since $\W$ is triangular. On a
flat manifold this is the ordinary Gaussian. On a curved one it is a density in tangent
coordinates, and it omits the volume factor of the retraction (for `SO3`,
$\det J(v) = 1 - \lVert v\rVert^2/12 + \cdots$). That is an approximation of the same order as the
Fisher root's below, and negligible for concentrated distributions.

`Tril` places no sign constraint on the diagonal of $\whiten$, and flipping the sign of a column of
$\whiten$ leaves $\Sigma$ unchanged. The absolute values make the density indifferent to it.

**The sampler matches the density.** `NormalSampler` draws $x = \mean \oplus \whiten z$ with
$z \sim \mathcal N(0, I_d)$. Since $(\mean \oplus \whiten z) \ominus \mean = \whiten z$ exactly,
$v = \whiten z \sim \mathcal N(0, \Sigma)$, which is the law above. So the Gaussian's term is the
log-density of its own sampler, and $q$ is the density of what `generate` draws
([The model graph](../graph/#sampling-the-generative-reading-of-q)).

## The residual and the normalizer

Split the log-density into its data-dependent and constant parts:
$$
-\log p = \tfrac12 \lVert r \rVert^2 - n(\whiten), \qquad
r = \W\,(x \ominus \mean), \qquad
n(\whiten) = -\tfrac{d}{2}\ln 2\pi + \ln\lvert\det \W\rvert .
$$
$r \in \R^d$ is the **whitened residual**. A least-squares estimator minimizes
$\tfrac12\sum\lVert r\rVert^2$ alone, which is the same problem only when no normalizer moves with
the quantities being estimated, that is, when $\whiten$ is not free. A Gaussian whose covariance is
estimated (heteroscedastic noise) is *not* a least-squares term, and the residual route refuses it
([Linearization and marginalization](../linearization/)).

`Normal`'s factor builds these nodes once, sharing $\W$, and binds them:

| Role | Node | For |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `RESIDUAL` | $r$ | Gauss–Newton rows ([Jacobians](../jacobians/#residual-rows)) |
| `LOCATION` | $\mean$ | a filter's $H = \partial\mean/\partial x$ |
| `WHITENING` | $\whiten$ | a filter's $R = \whiten\whiten\transpose$ |
| `NORMALIZER_PARENT` | $\whiten$ | the least-squares precondition above |
| `FISHER_ROOT` | $C$ | the Fisher information (below) |
| `ARGUMENT` | $x, \mean, \whiten$ | the columns $C$ is expressed over |

## The Fisher root

The Fisher information of a term with respect to its arguments $\eta = (x, \mean, \whiten)$ is the
expected outer product of its score. [Fisher scoring](../fisher-scoring/) explains why an estimator
wants it. Here we derive the matrix $C$ the Gaussian binds, with
$$
C\transpose C = \E_{x}\big[\nabla_\eta \log p\; \nabla_\eta \log p\transpose\big],
$$
over the $d + d + d(d+1)/2$ tangent coordinates of $(x, \mean, \whiten)$.

**Value and mean.** On a flat manifold $\partial r/\partial x = \W$ and
$\partial r / \partial \mean = -\W$, so the scores are $-\W\transpose r$ and $\W\transpose r$ with
$\E[rr\transpose] = I$. The block is $[\,\W\ \ -\W\,]\transpose [\,\W\ \ -\W\,]$.

**Covariance factor.** For packed coordinate $q$ of $\whiten$, let $E_q$ be the matrix with a one
at that entry. Then $\partial_q \Sigma = E_q\whiten\transpose + \whiten E_q\transpose$, and the
Gaussian's information over covariance parameters is
$F_{qq'} = \tfrac12 \operatorname{tr}\big(\Sigma^{-1}\partial_q\Sigma\,\Sigma^{-1}\partial_{q'}\Sigma\big)$.
With $G_q = \W E_q$,
$$
\Sigma^{-1}\partial_q\Sigma = \whiten^{-\top}\big(G_q + G_q\transpose\big)\whiten\transpose
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
F_{qq'} = \tfrac12\operatorname{tr}\big(S_q S_{q'}\big), \qquad S_q = G_q + G_q\transpose,
$$
by cyclicity of the trace. So the columns $\mathrm{vec}(S_q)/\sqrt2$ form a root $C_L$ with
$C_L\transpose C_L = F$.

**Cross terms** between the mean block and the covariance block are linear in $r$, and the odd
moments of a Gaussian vanish, so they are zero in expectation. The root is
$$
C = \begin{bmatrix} \W & -\W & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & C_L \end{bmatrix}
\in \R^{(d + d^2) \times (2d + d(d+1)/2)} .
$$
Every entry of $C$ is linear in $\W$, so $C = K\,\mathrm{vec}(\W)$ for one fixed, very sparse
matrix $K$, and the root is built as a `SparseLinearMap` of the packed whitening
([Operations on quantities](../quantity-operations/#skoro-linalg)). When $\whiten$ is a constant,
the whole root constant-folds.

**On a curved manifold** the value and mean blocks take $\partial r / \partial x$ and
$\partial r/\partial\mean$ to be $\pm\W$. On `SO3` and `SE3` that holds only at $r = 0$: the true
Jacobians carry $J(v)^{-1} = I + \tfrac12 v^\wedge + O(\lVert v\rVert^2)$ factors. The
corrections linear in $v$ average out, because $v$ has zero odd moments, so $C\transpose C$ is exact on
flat spaces and correct to second order in the dispersion on groups. Its relative error grows like
$\lVert\Sigma\rVert$, and vanishes when $\Sigma$ is isotropic. The measurements are on
[Fisher scoring](../fisher-scoring/#curved-manifolds).

## `GaussianBlock`

`GaussianBlock` is the read side: the view of a term that binds exactly one each of `RESIDUAL`,
`LOCATION` and `WHITENING`. It is derived from the bindings on every call, and never stored as a
second list that could drift.

## Joint factors

A distribution is a conditional over *one* quantity. A Gaussian over several quantities the model
already holds (for example, what marginalizing a quantity out leaves behind) is a
**`JointNormal`**, attached with `add_joint_factor` as a free-standing factor. Over quantities
$x_1, \dots, x_k$, with frozen anchors $a_i$, it scores
$$
\delta = (x_1 \ominus a_1; \dots; x_k \ominus a_k), \qquad
r = \W\,\delta - b, \qquad
\log \varphi = -\tfrac12\lVert r\rVert^2 + n,
$$
with $\W$ a constant `Tril` of the summed dimension, an optional constant offset $b$ (zero unless
`with_offset` gives one), and a constant normalizer $n$. It binds $r$ as its residual and $\W$
itself, unpacked, as its Fisher root: $C\transpose C = \W\transpose\W = \Lambda$, the precision of
$\delta$. The stacked tangent is what makes a joint over quantities on *different* manifolds
possible without a product manifold. Its dimension is the sum of the members' dimensions.

The block order of $\W$ must match the order of the quantities, and nothing can check that: a
`Tril` of the right width is a valid whitening for any ordering. A hand-built joint carries that
obligation. One built from a computed marginal does not
([Linearization and marginalization](../linearization/#joint-factors-from-marginals)).

## Is it the only distribution?

`Normal` is the only `Distribution` implemented in library code today. Anything non-Gaussian is
a bespoke implementation of the trait, and it may bind any subset of the roles.

